# Ziy — generated output is never committed.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: nothing here was ignored before this file, so the first
# `gh repo create --push` would have committed 323 MB of build output — including
# files of 177 MB, 53 MB and 44 MB that exceed GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit —
# and made every engine bump an unreviewable binary diff. See
# replica/compile-readiness.md §6 for the measurement.

# ── build output: regenerated by tools/build-shell.sh ────────────────────────
# Contains the packaged app dir (build/ziy/browser, firefox/, firefox-bin), the
# omni.ja patch logs and every built XPI. All of it is derived from tools/,
# shell/ and extensions/, which ARE tracked, so the source is already in the
# repo. Committing the output only guarantees disagreement with that source.
# 323 MB as of 2026-10-15.
build/

# ── extension packages: outputs, not inputs ─────────────────────────────────
# tools/build-shell.sh rebuilds these from extensions/*/. If an XPI ever needs to
# be committed deliberately, use `git add -f` on that one file.
*.xpi

# ── dependency trees ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# None are present today (checked 2026-10-15) — listed so a future
# `npm install`/`yarn` in extensions/ or server/ cannot sneak one into a commit.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# ── Rust build output + generated engine (native/ziy-blocks) ────────────────
# Same rule as build/: derived, never committed. `target/` is cargo's output;
# `dist/` holds the serialized adblock engine (7.5 MiB, rebuilt from the
# tracked snapshots by tools/build-blocks-engine.sh — see that script's header).
# Cargo.lock IS committed (binary crate: reproducible builds want it).
native/ziy-blocks/target/
native/ziy-blocks/dist/

# ── Ziy runtime/state dirs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# tools/bootstrap-profile.py writes profile state and the build writes scratch
# state here. Neither belongs in the repo.
.ziy/
shell/.ziy/

# ── reference screenshots of the ORIGINAL app (clean-room rule) ─────────────
# replica/screens/*.png (9 files, 1.5 MB) and replica/screens/behavior/*.png
# (4 files, 3.3 MB) are captures taken against the USER'S OWN Brave install
# (Brave Origin 153.1.95.104) over CDP on 2026-10-14, and MANIFEST.md says so on
# its second line: "design references only — never shipped". replica/evidence.md
# records them as OBSERVED-INSTALL and the clean-room rule there is explicit: no
# JS bundle decompiled, no .pak unpacked, "Ziy's own code, labels and design are
# clean-room".
#
# A private GitHub repo is still a repo: a second machine, other collaborators,
# and a permanent history. Copying another product's copyrighted UI captures into
# the repo's permanent history contradicts the very rule they exist to document,
# and git does not forgive — deleting them later leaves the blobs in every clone.
# So they are excluded BEFORE the first commit, exactly like build/ above.
# The MANIFEST and the proofs keep citing them; the files stay on disk in
# replica/screens/, untracked, where the design references are still readable.
replica/screens/*.png
replica/screens/behavior/

# ── Python ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# tools/lib/*.py are tracked; their caches are not.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.mypy cache and the like are not generated by these tools, but keep the rule:
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# ── OS / editor cruft ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# ── deliberately NOT ignored ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# patches/, tools/, shell/, extensions/, .github/, replica/*.md, brand/ —
# hand-written source and the proof documents. replica/screens/ IS ignored (see
# above): only its PNG files are, because those are captures of another
# product's UI; the MANIFEST.md and the proofs that cite them stay tracked.
